Fictional Band Name: Underfloor Weather

Band Description

Underfloor Weather is a five-member chamber-club ensemble built around the idea that dance music does not need a fast-moving bassline if the low-frequency foundation is physically large enough to become architecture.

Camille Duret — octobass, Floor Notes, slow bow-pressure design, acoustic low-frequency architecture.

Imani Cross — UK-garage / 2-step drums, swung percussion, Lift Gap arrangement, upper-room dynamics.

Noémie Gagnon — prepared celesta, suspended harmony, short high-register checkpoints.

Rashad Coleman — brushed snare, claves, acoustic transient substitutions, surface rhythm.

Leila Morgan — alto flute, breath texture, Lift Gap counterlines, key-noise articulation.

There is no conventional frontperson. Camille defines the floor; Imani decides how rapidly the room above it moves; Noémie supplies harmonic furniture that can shift without moving the foundation; Rashad adds tactile surface noise; Leila becomes most visible when the floor temporarily disappears. Live versions vary because hall size, floor construction, microphone distance, bow speed, and room modes radically change how one octobass note occupies physical space.

