Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Floor Holds Through Three Different Drums
- Story & Context: At 134 BPM, Camille begins one low octobass tonic half a bar before the first full two-step pattern arrives and sustains it across eight bars. Imani changes the kick placement three times during that single Floor Note: first a sparse offbeat pattern, then a busier broken pattern, then a near-empty two-kick cycle. Noémie changes from suspended-second celesta ticks to a minor-seventh dyad while the low note remains unchanged. Rashad replaces one programmed rim with clave on every fourth bar. Leila stays silent until the first Lift Gap, where Camille releases the bow for two bars and the entire room suddenly loses weight. The octobass return lands without a drum fill, making one sustained acoustic note feel larger than the previous eight bars of percussion.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Underground concrete floor plan with three different drum grids drawn above one uninterrupted thick blue line, enormous octobass scroll rising through a maintenance hatch, frost around pipe joints. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “three drums,” “same floor,” “lift for two,” and “still below.”

Track 2: Compressor Room Has No Substitute
- Story & Context: At 136 BPM, the track begins without octobass and deliberately refuses any electronic sub for sixteen bars. Imani uses the EP's most active hats and clipped kicks while Noémie plays dry celesta fifths that make the arrangement feel bright and almost weightless. Camille then enters on a bowed low fifth that lasts four bars; the perceived size of the track changes instantly even though no other channel gets louder. Midway through, a four-bar Lift Gap removes the low register again. Leila fills the space with breath-heavy alto-flute fourths, Rashad adds brush circles, and Imani resists the temptation to insert a bass drop. When Camille returns on a neighboring semitone, the harmonic shift feels structural because the previous floor had been absent long enough to be missed.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Empty compressor plinth with a warning label reading NO SUBSTITUTE, pressure-gauge needles suspended over a floor-level waveform that disappears for four marked bars, celesta keys reflected in a steel panel. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “no substitute,” “room gets light,” “one note returns,” and “pressure below.”

Track 3: Thaw Reading at 5:17 A.M.
- Story & Context: The closer runs at 130 BPM and uses the longest Floor Note on the record. Camille holds one low tonic for sixteen bars with minute bow-pressure changes while Imani gradually subtracts drums instead of adding them. Noémie moves through four upper harmonies over the same fundamental, showing how strongly the stable floor can reinterpret changing chords. Rashad uses only isolated brushed taps. Leila plays a slow descending line that becomes more exposed as hats disappear. In the final minute Camille releases the note and the band enters its longest Lift Gap. Nothing replaces the missing low end. After eight bars of celesta, flute, and sparse snare, Camille plays one final pizzicato octobass note rather than bowing it. The brief wooden impact decays naturally into the refrigeration room and ends the EP without a synthetic tail.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Analog thaw log stamped 5:17 A.M., a long low waveform running beneath four different chord diagrams before ending abruptly, condensation forming on blue pipes above an empty rink service corridor. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “five seventeen,” “thaw reading,” “floor released,” and “wood after silence.”

